1
00:00:00,102 --> 00:00:03,080
July seventeenth, nineteen forty-six.

2
00:00:03,880 --> 00:00:11,376
A burning airplane tears through a Beverly
Hills neighborhood, clips three houses,
and slams into the ground in a ball of
fire.

3
00:00:12,175 --> 00:00:15,712
Neighbors run toward the wreck, expecting
to pull out a body.

4
00:00:16,510 --> 00:00:22,248
Instead, they find a thin, tall man
crawling out of the flames, burned across
his chest,

5
00:00:22,383 --> 00:00:23,980
with his clothes on fire.

6
00:00:24,779 --> 00:00:29,892
He is one of the richest men in America,
and he has just crashed his own
experimental plane.

7
00:00:30,690 --> 00:00:32,496
His name is Howard Hughes.

8
00:00:33,294 --> 00:00:37,587
And the crash was only the beginning of a
story that would end in a sealed hotel
room,

9
00:00:37,773 --> 00:00:41,571
with a man who owned half of Las Vegas and
could not touch a doorknob.

10
00:00:42,486 --> 00:00:47,860
Howard Hughes was born on Christmas Eve,
nineteen oh-five, in Houston, Texas.

11
00:00:48,660 --> 00:00:53,643
His father, Howard Senior, had invented a
drill bit that could cut through hard
rock,

12
00:00:53,842 --> 00:00:55,674
and it changed the oil business.

13
00:00:56,473 --> 00:01:01,078
The Hughes Tool Company rented the bits
instead of selling them, and the money
poured in.

14
00:01:01,876 --> 00:01:04,750
Howard was a quiet boy who loved machines.

15
00:01:05,548 --> 00:01:09,606
By twelve, he had built a motorized
bicycle from spare parts.

16
00:01:10,405 --> 00:01:12,901
When he was sixteen, his mother died.

17
00:01:13,699 --> 00:01:16,351
Two years later, his father died too.

18
00:01:17,150 --> 00:01:19,893
The drill bit was a small miracle of
engineering.

19
00:01:20,691 --> 00:01:25,466
Before it, drilling through hard rock
could take weeks and destroy many cheap
bits.

20
00:01:26,264 --> 00:01:30,895
The new bit, with its cones of steel
teeth, bit through rock far faster.

21
00:01:31,694 --> 00:01:36,442
Oil companies around the world wanted it,
and they were willing to pay rent on it
forever.

22
00:01:37,241 --> 00:01:40,492
At eighteen, he was an orphan with a giant
company.

23
00:01:41,290 --> 00:01:44,007
A court allowed him to take control at
nineteen.

24
00:01:44,806 --> 00:01:47,927
Relatives expected him to sell it and
enjoy his money.

25
00:01:48,725 --> 00:01:50,323
Hughes did something else.

26
00:01:51,121 --> 00:01:56,651
He kept the tool company, which would fund
every dream he ever had, and he moved to
Hollywood.

27
00:01:57,582 --> 00:02:01,627
Hughes wanted to make movies, and he
wanted them bigger than anyone else.

28
00:02:02,428 --> 00:02:07,723
His first film, Hell's Angels, was a war
epic about pilots in the First World War.

29
00:02:08,522 --> 00:02:13,622
He bought dozens of real planes, hired
hundreds of pilots, and spent years
filming.

30
00:02:14,420 --> 00:02:19,637
The budget exploded to nearly four million
dollars, an astonishing sum in nineteen
thirty.

31
00:02:20,436 --> 00:02:27,567
During filming, a pilot died in a crash,
and Hughes himself nearly died when he
flew a plane for a scene and crashed.

32
00:02:28,365 --> 00:02:29,585
He did not stop.

33
00:02:30,384 --> 00:02:34,663
When sound movies arrived, he scrapped
most of the film and shot it again.

34
00:02:35,462 --> 00:02:36,773
The movie was a hit.

35
00:02:37,571 --> 00:02:41,538
It also launched Jean Harlow, one of
Hollywood's biggest stars.

36
00:02:42,337 --> 00:02:47,619
Hughes then produced Scarface, a violent
gangster film that was banned in several
states.

37
00:02:48,418 --> 00:02:54,689
Hughes also dated some of the most famous
actresses in the world, including
Katharine Hepburn and Ava Gardner.

38
00:02:55,488 --> 00:02:59,833
Newspapers followed him everywhere, and he
learned how to use the press as a weapon,

39
00:03:00,019 --> 00:03:01,695
and also how to hide from it.

40
00:03:02,493 --> 00:03:07,320
He became a Hollywood legend before he was
thirty, and he still was not satisfied.

41
00:03:08,118 --> 00:03:10,080
What he really loved was flying.

42
00:03:10,998 --> 00:03:14,614
In nineteen thirty-two, Hughes started an
aircraft company.

43
00:03:15,414 --> 00:03:18,118
He wanted to build the fastest plane in
the world.

44
00:03:18,917 --> 00:03:24,212
He sat in the design office, drawing and
redrawing, and obsessing over every rivet.

45
00:03:25,011 --> 00:03:30,983
He insisted that the rivets be flush with
the skin so the airplane would cut through
the air with almost no drag.

46
00:03:31,781 --> 00:03:40,358
In nineteen thirty-five, he flew the
Hughes H-1 racer, and he set a new world
speed record of three hundred and fifty-
two miles per hour.

47
00:03:41,156 --> 00:03:45,397
Two years later, he flew coast to coast in
just over seven hours.

48
00:03:46,196 --> 00:03:50,814
Then, in nineteen thirty-eight, he did
something that made him a national hero.

49
00:03:51,612 --> 00:03:58,965
He flew around the world in three days,
nineteen hours, and seventeen minutes,
beating the old record by more than half.

50
00:03:59,763 --> 00:04:02,038
New York gave him a ticker tape parade.

51
00:04:02,836 --> 00:04:05,814
The pilot who loved speed was also an
engineer.

52
00:04:06,612 --> 00:04:08,783
He tested every design himself.

53
00:04:09,581 --> 00:04:15,788
Colleagues said he knew every wire in his
planes, and that he could spot a flaw in a
blueprint from across the room.

54
00:04:16,586 --> 00:04:20,749
He was thirty-two years old, handsome,
rich, and famous.

55
00:04:21,547 --> 00:04:23,288
Nothing seemed impossible.

56
00:04:24,198 --> 00:04:30,483
During the Second World War, the United
States government hired Hughes to build
two enormous flying machines.

57
00:04:31,284 --> 00:04:33,584
One was a photo-reconnaissance plane.

58
00:04:34,382 --> 00:04:41,097
The other was a gigantic wooden flying
boat designed to carry soldiers across the
Atlantic and avoid German submarines.

59
00:04:41,896 --> 00:04:43,962
The press called it the Spruce Goose.

60
00:04:44,760 --> 00:04:46,566
The project was a disaster.

61
00:04:47,364 --> 00:04:51,110
It was late, expensive, and almost
impossible to build.

62
00:04:51,909 --> 00:04:57,855
By nineteen forty-seven, the Senate was
investigating Hughes for wasting millions
in war contracts.

63
00:04:58,653 --> 00:05:04,860
In front of cameras, Hughes fought back,
shouting at senators, and the hearings
became a spectacle.

64
00:05:05,659 --> 00:05:10,446
Then, on November second, nineteen forty-
seven, he did the impossible.

65
00:05:11,244 --> 00:05:16,384
He climbed into the giant boat, with
reporters on board, and flew it for about
a mile,

66
00:05:16,557 --> 00:05:19,378
a few feet above the water of Long Beach
harbor.

67
00:05:20,177 --> 00:05:23,792
The Spruce Goose, which everyone had
mocked, had flown.

68
00:05:24,591 --> 00:05:27,477
He said that if it failed, he would leave
the country.

69
00:05:28,276 --> 00:05:31,266
It did not fail, and yet he never flew it
again.

70
00:05:32,065 --> 00:05:38,206
In nineteen forty-six, Hughes was testing
the XF-eleven, a fast reconnaissance plane
for the Army.

71
00:05:39,005 --> 00:05:43,714
Minutes into the flight, a propeller
malfunctioned, and the plane lost control.

72
00:05:44,513 --> 00:05:48,818
He tried to reach a golf course, failed,
and crashed into the houses.

73
00:05:49,617 --> 00:05:51,358
Doctors said he would not live.

74
00:05:52,156 --> 00:05:56,149
He had crushed ribs, a collapsed lung, and
severe burns.

75
00:05:56,948 --> 00:06:04,092
Hughes survived, but he spent months in
bed, and he asked for a special hospital
bed with controls he could operate
himself.

76
00:06:04,890 --> 00:06:08,949
Engineers built it, and it became the
model for modern hospital beds.

77
00:06:09,870 --> 00:06:16,533
After the crash in nineteen forty-six,
Hughes was in terrible pain, and he began
taking strong painkillers.

78
00:06:17,333 --> 00:06:21,222
His mind, which had always been intense,
started to change.

79
00:06:22,021 --> 00:06:24,035
He became obsessed with germs.

80
00:06:24,833 --> 00:06:33,201
He demanded that his aides wear white
gloves, open doors with tissues, and
follow written instructions of hundreds of
pages to open a can of fruit.

81
00:06:33,999 --> 00:06:37,069
At the same time, he was building another
empire.

82
00:06:37,867 --> 00:06:42,069
In nineteen forty-eight, he bought control
of the movie studio RKO.

83
00:06:42,867 --> 00:06:49,569
In nineteen sixty-six, he sold his shares
in the airline TWA for about five hundred
and fifty million dollars,

84
00:06:49,794 --> 00:06:52,342
one of the largest personal payouts in
history.

85
00:06:53,140 --> 00:06:58,488
Then he arrived in Las Vegas, took the top
floor of a hotel, and refused to leave.

86
00:06:59,286 --> 00:07:02,928
When the owners tried to throw him out, he
simply bought the hotel.

87
00:07:03,726 --> 00:07:05,155
Then he kept buying.

88
00:07:05,953 --> 00:07:09,647
Casinos, land, even a local television
station.

89
00:07:10,445 --> 00:07:17,095
His company, Hughes Aircraft, became a
pillar of the American military, building
missiles, radar,

90
00:07:17,255 --> 00:07:18,345
and satellites.

91
00:07:19,143 --> 00:07:26,535
In nineteen seventy-four, the Central
Intelligence Agency used his name as cover
to build a giant ship called the Glomar
Explorer,

92
00:07:26,721 --> 00:07:31,379
which secretly tried to raise a sunken
Soviet submarine from the bottom of the
Pacific.

93
00:07:32,177 --> 00:07:37,147
At his peak, Hughes was one of the richest
men in the world, and he lived like a
prisoner.

94
00:07:38,070 --> 00:07:43,964
In his last years, Hughes lived in
blacked-out hotel rooms, in Las Vegas,
Nassau,

95
00:07:44,151 --> 00:07:46,087
Vancouver, and Acapulco.

96
00:07:46,887 --> 00:07:49,032
He rarely bathed or cut his hair.

97
00:07:49,830 --> 00:07:54,865
He wrote long memos about his phobias, and
he watched the same movies over and over.

98
00:07:55,663 --> 00:08:00,125
Late in life, aides described a man who
spent days naked in a darkened room,

99
00:08:00,299 --> 00:08:04,227
living on chocolate bars and milk, and
watching movies on a projector.

100
00:08:05,025 --> 00:08:06,688
His fingernails grew long.

101
00:08:07,486 --> 00:08:13,055
He was still signing deals, buying hotels,
and running a business empire by
telephone,

102
00:08:13,281 --> 00:08:15,360
through a small circle of loyal men.

103
00:08:16,158 --> 00:08:22,209
He died on April fifth, nineteen seventy-
six, on a plane taking him to a hospital
in Houston.

104
00:08:23,007 --> 00:08:24,956
He weighed about ninety pounds.

105
00:08:25,755 --> 00:08:28,745
The doctors had to use fingerprints to
identify him.

106
00:08:29,544 --> 00:08:33,016
He had no wife, no children, and no clear
will.

107
00:08:33,814 --> 00:08:38,758
A handwritten document called the Mormon
will appeared, leaving a fortune to
unlikely heirs,

108
00:08:38,932 --> 00:08:40,802
but a court declared it a forgery.

109
00:08:41,601 --> 00:08:45,789
After years of fights, his estate was
divided among distant cousins.

110
00:08:46,588 --> 00:08:52,000
Years later, a Hollywood director made a
film about him, and a new generation
learned his name.

111
00:08:52,799 --> 00:08:58,485
But the real story is stranger than the
movies, because Howard Hughes was not just
a rich eccentric.

112
00:08:59,283 --> 00:09:02,495
He was also one of the great builders of
the twentieth century.

113
00:09:03,294 --> 00:09:07,938
The fortune, worth well over a billion
dollars, had been built on a drill bit.

114
00:09:08,736 --> 00:09:13,680
But the man who inherited it had spent his
life trying to outrun something he could
never escape.

115
00:09:14,598 --> 00:09:17,107
Howard Hughes lived a spectacular life.

116
00:09:17,908 --> 00:09:22,864
He broke records, produced hit movies, and
built machines that dazzled the world.

117
00:09:23,663 --> 00:09:27,422
And yet, he died alone, afraid of an
invisible enemy.

118
00:09:28,220 --> 00:09:30,156
His story has two lessons.

119
00:09:30,954 --> 00:09:34,036
First, genius without limits can become a
trap.

120
00:09:34,835 --> 00:09:38,268
Hughes could buy anything, so no one could
ever tell him no.

121
00:09:39,066 --> 00:09:41,510
Second, a fortune is not freedom.

122
00:09:42,309 --> 00:09:46,979
At the end, the richest man in the world
was a man who could not walk through a
door.

123
00:09:47,777 --> 00:09:53,593
If you enjoyed this story, subscribe,
because tomorrow at six, another file
opens.

